+# Daily Grind origin migration
+
+Run `npm run build`. Deploy `dist/old` at `https://old-app.example.com`
+and `dist/new` at `https://new-app.example.com`, each as its own site root.
+These are placeholder origins: replace them in both manifests, the association
+file, the fallback link in `index.html`, and the validation in `scripts/build.mjs`
+before deployment. Both origins must use HTTPS and share the same registrable
+domain (eTLD+1); do not infer it by taking the last two hostname labels.
+
+The old manifest's `id` must match the identity of the already installed app.
+The predecessor shown here is `https://old-app.example.com/`; change it to the
+actual installed identity if different. The successor identity resolves to
+`https://new-app.example.com/`, and its install page links to `/manifest.json`.
+
+Serve `/manifest.json` with `application/manifest+json` (or `application/json`).
+On the old origin, serve `/.well-known/web-app-origin-association` as public JSON
+with `application/json`, without authentication, redirects, or SPA fallback.
+Ensure the host serves dot-directories. The authorization includes both the
+successor origin and its exact manifest URL. Keep the old host available so the
+browser can verify the handover and existing users can follow the fallback link.
+
+Migration is suggested by default. It preserves installation integration such
+as shortcuts, not local storage, IndexedDB, cookies, or browser permissions.
+Users are told to sign in and configure their settings again. Add a separate
+authenticated data synchronization flow if data continuity is needed.
+Browsers without migration support can use the always-visible old-app banner
+to open and manually install the new app, then remove the old installation.
